//
// @file builtin.rho
// @brief What the compiler supplies without an import, written down.
//
// This module declares nothing. The primitive types and the built-in functions
// are the compiler's own: each backend spells them (C through the runtime it
// writes beside the program, Go through its own types and the generated
// helpers), so a declaration here could only be a second, competing meaning for
// a name the compiler already knows. An earlier version declared them — `type
// u32`, `extern fn print` — and importing it replaced the primitives with
// opaque types of the same name, so `int_to_str(u32(n))` was refused with
// "argument 1 has type u32, want u32".
//
// Importing it is allowed and changes nothing, on every backend.
//
// The primitive types:
//
//	bool
//	u8 u16 u32 u64       unsigned integers of that many bits
//	i8 i16 i32 i64       signed integers, two's complement
//	f32 f64              IEEE 754 floating point
//	string               the language's text, a literal or a runtime string
//	Ptr[T]               the language's pointer (functions over it: std.core.ptr)
//	T[N]                 N values of T, the length part of the type
//
// The built-in functions — `print`, `abort`, `panic`, `strlen`, `str_eq`,
// `int_to_str`, `sqrt` and the rest — are listed with their signatures and the
// backends that supply them on the built-in functions page of the language
// reference (docs/language/builtins.md).
//
// @project Rho Programming Language
// @version 0.2
// @date 2026
//
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
//

module std.core.builtin
